    {
      "id": "ff38b11c-2bc3-4ec2-b573-4e4ef8106a55",
      "name": "MCP makes context useful",
      "path": "WWW / MCP Journal / MCP makes context useful",
      "docTypeId": "683e8a6c-7d96-4a93-8263-134a850ae11e",
      "templateId": "66a5e32f-85f5-41b3-a0fe-1d551861e895",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "blocks",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "70715d50-c1de-46f1-8f18-b126f81c8249",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "f90111fa-c132-411b-99d6-dd91decaa6a3",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "23fb5c26-d5c9-4881-a54a-57ec77c82724",
                "key": "70715d50-c1de-46f1-8f18-b126f81c8249",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "kicker",
                    "value": "FIELD NOTE 01"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "heading",
                    "value": "MCP makes context useful, not just available."
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "summary",
                    "value": "The best assistant is not the one with the longest prompt. It is the one that can reach the right context and act on it at the right time."
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "5d58714e-7983-491f-a3f5-bb8c57cc9cca",
                        "mediaKey": "0b886a49-ba67-4a77-8247-a541e4153f6d",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "imageCredit",
                    "value": "Photo by Chris Ried on Unsplash"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "464928fd-135b-4d92-a7cb-878006c108ae",
                "key": "f90111fa-c132-411b-99d6-dd91decaa6a3",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "heading",
                    "value": "From copied context to live context"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "body",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>Working with an AI assistant often starts with a familiar ritual: copy a document, paste a log, explain where the data came from, and hope the details stay current. It works for a quick question, but it gets fragile as soon as the work spans several tools.</p><p>The Model Context Protocol (MCP) gives an assistant a consistent way to discover resources and call tools. A project can expose its own content model, a design system can expose its components, and a tracker can expose the work in progress. The assistant can ask for the piece it needs when it needs it.</p><h2>Context with a source</h2><p>A useful answer has a trail. When context comes from a named resource or a tool response, people can see what the assistant used and check whether it is still valid. That is much easier to trust than a large, anonymous paste in a prompt.</p><h2>Action with structure</h2><p>MCP tools describe their inputs. Instead of guessing at a free-form command, an assistant can submit a typed request and read the result back. In this demo, the same local connection creates content types, imports images, publishes posts, and verifies the saved block grid. Each step has a concrete response.</p><p>The exciting part is practical: less setup between an idea and a working result, with the source of truth still in the system that owns it.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "70715d50-c1de-46f1-8f18-b126f81c8249",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "f90111fa-c132-411b-99d6-dd91decaa6a3",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "urls": [
        "/mcp-makes-context-useful/"
      ],
