    {
      "id": "1f0b10ae-0a50-4570-9ce0-03faaa81405c",
      "name": "One protocol, many possibilities",
      "path": "WWW / MCP Journal / One protocol, many possibilities",
      "docTypeId": "683e8a6c-7d96-4a93-8263-134a850ae11e",
      "templateId": "66a5e32f-85f5-41b3-a0fe-1d551861e895",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "blocks",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "1b896b2d-23d7-4b7c-93a9-87a3f1496914",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "69dd391f-d755-424d-9cd3-2b858a9b09a4",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "23fb5c26-d5c9-4881-a54a-57ec77c82724",
                "key": "1b896b2d-23d7-4b7c-93a9-87a3f1496914",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "kicker",
                    "value": "FIELD NOTE 02"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "heading",
                    "value": "One protocol, many possibilities."
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "summary",
                    "value": "MCP turns integrations into reusable building blocks, so a good connection can help across many tasks instead of just one scripted demo."
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "6f60b06a-32ee-4417-9f67-88d1b53b4826",
                        "mediaKey": "4ed6c0f0-4241-4df3-9cfa-c12f3952fd0b",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "imageCredit",
                    "value": "Photo by Vardan Papikyan on Unsplash"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "464928fd-135b-4d92-a7cb-878006c108ae",
                "key": "69dd391f-d755-424d-9cd3-2b858a9b09a4",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "heading",
                    "value": "A shared language for useful tools"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "body",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>Every useful workflow crosses boundaries. A writer needs research and a publishing tool. A developer needs source code and an issue tracker. A site editor needs content types, media, and a way to publish. Traditionally each pairing calls for its own custom glue.</p><p>MCP offers a shared interface for those connections. A server describes what it can do; a client discovers those capabilities and uses them through the same protocol. The value is not a single magic command. It is the growing set of small, understandable actions that can be combined.</p><h2>Build once, use in new ways</h2><p>A media import tool can support an editorial workflow today and a content audit tomorrow. A document type tool can help with a prototype, then help verify a migration. The interface is reusable because the capability belongs to the server, not to one prompt.</p><h2>Keep people in the loop</h2><p>Good tools return clear results. That makes it possible to inspect a change, spot a failed validation, and adjust the next step. The workflow here used that loop repeatedly: model the schema, create content, read it back, and only then build the views.</p><p>That is why MCP feels promising: it helps tools meet each other on common ground while leaving the underlying systems in charge of their own data.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "1b896b2d-23d7-4b7c-93a9-87a3f1496914",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "69dd391f-d755-424d-9cd3-2b858a9b09a4",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "urls": [
        "/one-protocol-many-possibilities/"
      ],
